This code sets up a Raspberry Pi 4 Model B to read CPU temperature and CPU usage percentage, display them on an SSD1306 128×64 I2C OLED display, and handles errors for I2C communication and file operations. It uses pigpio for I2C control and basic C functions for OLED display. The program runs in an infinite loop, updating the display every second. It also includes a SIGINT handler to cleanly exit.

Please note that you need to have the pigpio library installed and properly configured on your Raspberry Pi. Also, the OLED display should be connected correctly to the I2C bus 1.

This code assumes that the OLED display is connected to the Raspberry Pi's I2C bus 1. If it's connected to a different bus, you'll need to adjust the `OLED_ADDRESS` constant accordingly.

The bitmap font used here is a simple 5x7 font. You can replace it with any other bitmap font supported by the pigpio library.

The error handling in this code is basic and includes printing error messages to stderr and exiting gracefully. In a production environment, you might want to implement more sophisticated error handling and logging mechanisms.

