This code sets up an I2C interface to a Silicon Labs Si1102 proximity sensor on I2C bus 1, configures the sensor's interrupt to GPIO27 on a rising edge, and reads the proximity value on each interrupt. It compares the value to a configurable threshold and appends a log entry to "proximity.log" with the timestamp, raw proximity reading, and status ("SAFE" or "ALERT"). The program accepts optional I2C address and threshold as command-line arguments, initializes the I2C and GPIO interfaces with error checking, and implements a thread-safe interrupt handler that does not perform blocking calls. It also installs a SIGINT handler to cleanly shut down the sensor driver on receipt of a SIGINT signal.

Please note that this code assumes the use of pigpio for GPIO control and I2C communication. Make sure to install pigpio and link it during compilation.

To compile the code, use:

```bash
gcc -o proximity_sensor proximity_sensor.c -lpigpio -lrt
```

Before running, ensure that the user running the program has write permissions to the log file and the I2C device file.

