This code sets up a Raspberry Pi GPIO PWM for a common-cathode RGB LED, using the pigpio library for software PWM. It runs in an infinite loop, generating random intensity values for each color channel every 100 ms and changing the color randomly every 100 ms. The program handles errors using the `perror` function and exits cleanly on receipt of a SIGINT signal (Ctrl+C). The `clock_nanosleep` function is used for sub-millisecond precision timing.

Please note that you need to install pigpio library and have the necessary permissions to access GPIO pins. You can install pigpio using `sudo apt-get install pigpio`.

Before running the code, make sure to replace the GPIO pin numbers (PIN_RED, PIN_GREEN, PIN_BLUE) with the actual physical pin numbers on your Raspberry Pi.

Also, ensure that the pigpio library is properly installed and accessible in your system's library path.

